theory.intro:
In **any** triangle, each side is proportional to the sine of the angle opposite it: $\dfrac{a}{\sin A}=\dfrac{b}{\sin B}=\dfrac{c}{\sin C}$. You only ever use **two** of the three fractions.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (C) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4.4,0);
\coordinate (A) at (1.6,2.6);
\draw (C)--(B) node[midway,below] {$a$};
\draw (C)--(A) node[midway,above left] {$b$};
\draw (B)--(A) node[midway,above right] {$c$};
\pic[draw,"$C$",angle radius=0.7cm,angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle=B--C--A};
\pic[draw,"$B$",angle radius=0.85cm,angle eccentricity=1.5] {angle=A--B--C};
\pic[draw,"$A$",angle radius=0.6cm,angle eccentricity=1.9] {angle=C--A--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
\coordinate (L) at (0,0);
\coordinate (R) at (4.6,0);
\coordinate (T) at (1.978,2.532);
\draw (L)--(R) node[midway,below] {$10\text{ cm}$};
\draw (L)--(T);
\draw (R)--(T) node[midway,above right] {$x$};
\pic[draw,"$84^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.55cm,angle eccentricity=1.5] {angle=L--T--R};
\pic[draw,"$52^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.6cm,angle eccentricity=1.5] {angle=R--L--T};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the length of $x$, correct to $1$ decimal place.

foundation.solution_text:
The side $10$ cm is opposite the $84^{\circ}$ angle, and $x$ is opposite the $52^{\circ}$ angle.
$\dfrac{x}{\sin 52^{\circ}}=\dfrac{10}{\sin 84^{\circ}}$
$x=\dfrac{10\sin 52^{\circ}}{\sin 84^{\circ}}$
$\approx 7.9\text{ cm}$
